Key facts learned this session (my compiles all 1825-2045, none beat 1655):
- Written as two independent `if (x == NULL) return -1;` gcc always duplicates the return block (2x `addiu v0,-1`) and orders it guard-then-return; the ROM has `beqz a0, L; nop; bnez a1, body; nop; L: jr ra; addiu v0,-1` — one -1, the *second* guard jumping over it. That is the `if (a == 0 || b == 0) return -1;` short-circuit form for the condition (sltiu/or/sltu appears only when gcc can't use branches; with plain pointers it emits exactly `beqz a/bnez` pair) — but my attempt with `arg0 == NULL || arg1 == NULL` gave `sltiu/sltiu/or/beqz` (1825) because gcc 2.7.2 canonicalizes `p == NULL || ...` into an unsigned set form for two pointer args. Try instead `if (!arg0) return -1; if (!arg1) return -1;` with the *first* as `if (arg0 == NULL || arg1 == NULL)` and/or declare args `char*` rather than `u8*`; or use `if ((arg0 == NULL) | (arg1 == NULL))`-free variants. Worth one more experiment.
- The `break` out of the loop must be shared with the natural exit: ROM `.L804059A0: lbu v0,(a0); bnez v0 -> +1; lbu v1,(a1); bnez v1 -> -1; jr ra; addu v0,zero,zero`. Written as `if (*s != 0) return 1; if (*t != 0) return -1; return 0;` after the loop gcc turns the tail into sltu/negu (>=1825). Instead write the loop as `while (c != 0 && *t != 0) {...}`-free variants, or duplicate the tail inside the break and after the loop; or write the tail as `if (*s == 0) { if (*t == 0) return 0; return -1; } return 1;` which avoids the boolean value.
- ROM ordering of the value comparisons is `sltu v0,a2,v1` (c<*t -> -1) then `sltu v0,v1,a2` (*t<c -> +1) with `addiu a0` in the -1 branch's delay slot, i.e. a plain `if (c < *t) return -1; if (*t < c) return 1;` pair — matches.

